---
<Base title="What Is Fungal Research?" description="Discover the many branches of modern mycology, independent community science projects, and ways to contribute to real fungal research.">
<section class="page-hero container"><p class="eyebrow">INVESTIGATE / FIND YOUR WAY IN</p><h1>What is fungal research?</h1><p class="lead">It's much bigger than identifying mushrooms. It's the ongoing effort to understand fungal life—and there are more ways to take part than you might imagine.</p><p><a class="button primary" href="#fit">Find your kind of question ↓</a></p></section>
<section class="section soft"><div class="container narrow"><p class="eyebrow">THE BIG PICTURE</p><h2>A kingdom we have barely begun to document.</h2><p>Fungi shape ecosystems, agriculture, medicine, food webs and the evolution of life. Yet much of their diversity remains undescribed, and many habitats are poorly sampled. Modern mycology spans field biology, taxonomy, evolutionary genetics, microscopy, physiology, ecology, conservation, genomics and more.</p><p>The challenge is not simply that fungi are hard to see. Research and conservation also face shortages of specialists, collections infrastructure, training opportunities and funding. A 2025 scientific declaration highlights these constraints; a 2024 review specifically argues that citizen science can help address major fungal conservation data gaps.</p><p><strong>An important distinction:</strong> evidence supports chronic underinvestment and taxonomic neglect, but not a universal claim that all mycology funding is currently declining. Molecular research and fungal conservation initiatives are growing in some areas even as traditional taxonomy and documentation remain stretched.</p><div class="research-reading"><a href="https://doi.org/10.1111/conl.13013" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read: The power of citizen science to advance fungal conservation (2024) ↗</a><a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/ppp3.10569" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read: The 2030 Declaration on Scientific Plant and Fungal Collecting (2025) ↗</a></div></div></section>
<section class="section" id="ways"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">MODERN MYCOLOGY</p><h2>Six kinds of questions you could help answer.</h2><p class="resource-intro">These are overlapping research activities, not exclusive career tracks. You can start with an observation and end up collaborating on a species description—or contribute valuable data without ever publishing a paper.</p><p class="mine-legend"><Image src={logo} alt="" width={20} densities={[1,2]} format="webp" /> marks the kinds of research I’m actively doing myself. <a href="#closer">Here’s what that looks like ↓</a></p><div class="fungal-fields">{areas.map(a=><article class={a.mine?'fungal-field is-mine':'fungal-field'}><div class="fungal-field-top"><span class="fungal-field-number">{a.number}</span>{a.mine&&<span class="mine-badge"><Image src={logo} alt="" width={18} densities={[1,2]} format="webp" />I do this</span>}</div><h3>{a.title}</h3><p class="fungal-field-question">{a.question}</p><p><strong>What you can do:</strong> {a.action}</p><p class="fungal-field-next">{a.lead} {a.links.map(([label,href],i)=><>{i>0&&(i===a.links.length-1?' and ':', ')}<a href={href} {...(href.startsWith('http')?{target:'_blank',rel:'noopener noreferrer'}:{})}>{label}</a></>)}{a.tail&&` ${a.tail}`}{a.links.length>0&&!a.tail&&'.'}</p></article>)}</div><p class="resource-footnote">Looking for a more precise distinction between taxonomy, systematics, phylogenetics and nomenclature? <a href="/investigate/resources/">Read the discipline guide →</a></p></div></section>
<section class="section soft"><div class="container narrow"><p class="eyebrow">WHY COMMUNITY SCIENCE MATTERS</p><h2>More eyes, more places, more questions.</h2><p>Professional researchers cannot survey every forest, examine every collection or monitor every season. Community scientists can contribute geographic reach, repeat observations, local ecological knowledge, collections and specialized skills. Research partnerships can turn that work into data useful for taxonomy, conservation and ecology.</p><p>But participation is not a substitute for rigor. Useful observations need context; sequence matches need verification; conservation claims need defensible methods. Nor should volunteer enthusiasm be used as an excuse to underfund professional scientists, collections and laboratories. The strongest work is collaborative—and gives people appropriate training, access and credit.</p></div></section>
<section class="section" id="projects"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">PEOPLE BUILDING NEW PATHWAYS</p><h2>Community science is already happening.</h2><p class="resource-intro">These are examples of independent and collaborative initiatives—not an exhaustive ranking, and not all open to every volunteer at all times.</p><div class="fungal-initiatives">{initiatives.map((p,i)=><article class="fungal-initiative"><div class="fungal-person">{people[i].photo?<img src={people[i].photo} alt={`Portrait of ${people[i].names}`} loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer" />:<a class="fungal-photo-pending" href={people[i].profile} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label={`View official photographs and team information for ${people[i].names}`}><span aria-hidden="true">◎</span><small>View the people behind the project ↗</small></a>}<div class="fungal-person-caption"><strong>{people[i].names}</strong><span>{people[i].role}</span>{people[i].photoCredit&&<small>{people[i].photoCredit}</small>}</div></div><p class="eyebrow">{p.tag}</p><h3>{p.name}</h3><p class="fungal-field-question">{p.focus}</p><p>{p.description}</p><p><strong>Where you might fit:</strong> {p.participate}</p><a href={p.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{p.link} ↗</a></article>)}</div><p class="resource-footnote">Photographs shown here are editorial mockups for review, not yet licensed for republication. Credits identify the source, not permission. For projects without a confidently identified photograph, the card links to the project team rather than showing unrelated people.</p></div></section>
<section class="section closer" id="closer"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">CLOSER TO HOME</p><h2>What this looks like for me.</h2><p class="resource-intro">[Short intro in your voice: I started as a podcaster who liked mushrooms, and ended up sequencing them. Here’s the path, and where it’s going next.]</p><ol class="closer-steps">{story.map(c=><li class="closer-step">{c.draft&&<span class="draft-flag">Draft</span>}<p class="eyebrow">{c.tag}</p><h3>{c.title}</h3><p class="closer-status">{c.status}</p>{c.body.map(t=><p>{t}</p>)}{'youtubeId' in c&&(c.youtubeId?<div class="closer-video"><iframe src={`https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/${c.youtubeId}`} title={`${c.title} video`} loading="lazy" allow="accelerometer; encrypted-media; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe></div>:<div class="closer-video is-pending" aria-hidden="true"><span>▶</span><small>Video coming soon</small></div>)}{c.link&&<a href={c.link[1]} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{c.link[0]} ↗</a>}</li>)}</ol></div></section>
<section class="section soft" id="fit"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">WHERE DO YOU FIT?</p><h2>Start with what you already enjoy.</h2><p class="resource-intro">You don't need to choose a title. Choose a question or activity worth returning to.</p><div class="fungal-paths">{paths.map(p=><details><summary>{p.title}</summary><p><strong>Try this first:</strong> {p.start}</p><p><strong>Go further:</strong> {p.grow}</p></details>)}</div><p class="research-invitation">A useful scientific contribution might be a carefully documented mushroom, a corrected record, a specimen someone can reexamine, a question that changes a study—or a collaboration you helped make possible.</p><div class="fungal-cta"><a class="button primary" href="/investigate/resources/">Explore research resources →</a><a class="button" href="/investigate/tools/">Open the Research Workbench →</a></div></div></section>
</Base>
